{"maxId":256,"entries":{"chapter-59-study-dementia-including-alzheimer-disease|Chapter 59 Study: Dementia Including Alzheimer Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|AD = most common neurodegenerative disorder / cause of dementia in older adults.":["u3"],"copy|>6 million Americans, >44 million worldwide have AD or a related dementia (p894).":["u4"],"copy|Prevalence rises steeply with age: ~3% (65–74y), 17% (75–84y), 32% (85y+) (p894).":["u5"],"copy|Late-onset (sporadic) AD = >95% of all AD cases (p894); familial (younger-onset, <65y) AD = 1–5% (p897).":["u6"],"copy|Median survival from diagnosis to death: 7–10 years (p901).":["u7"],"copy|AD is the most common dementia in the United States, accounting for 50–90% of all dementia cases (p907).":["u8"],"copy|Familial AD genes: APP, PSEN1, PSEN2. PSEN1 = 78% of familial AD mutations (most common) (p897); familial AD onset typically late 40s–early 50s (p898).":["u9"],"copy|APOE ε4: one copy → 4× risk, two copies → 12× risk of AD (p895). Framingham: 55% of ε4 homozygotes vs 27% heterozygotes vs 9% noncarriers developed AD by age 85 (p895). ε2 allele is protective.":["u10"],"copy|Vascular risk factors (high midlife cholesterol/BP, diabetes, metabolic syndrome, obesity, inactivity) all raise AD risk. Midlife obesity RR 1.60 (95% CI 1.34–1.92); midlife physical inactivity RR 1.82 (95% CI 1.19–2.78) (p896).":["u11"],"copy|About 1/3 of AD cases worldwide might be attributable to potentially modifiable risk factors (p896).":["u12"],"copy|TBI: WWII veterans with moderate TBI had 2× AD risk, severe TBI with loss of consciousness 4× (p896).":["u13"],"copy|Late-life depression history → AD risk OR 1.65 (95% CI 1.42–1.92) (p896); >30% of AD patients develop depression during the illness (may be the first symptom).":["u14"],"copy|Low educational attainment: pooled RR 1.80 (95% CI 1.45–2.27) for AD (p897).":["u15"],"copy|Hallmarks: amyloid (Aβ) plaques + neurofibrillary tangles (tau); neuritic plaques = subset with dystrophic neurites.":["u16"],"copy|Cerebral amyloid angiopathy co-occurs in 80–90% of AD patients (sporadic CAA) — can cause lobar hemorrhage/microbleeds (p898).":["u17"],"copy|2012 NIA-AA neuropathologic \"ABC score\" (Amyloid/Braak/CERAD) grades autopsy findings as none/low/intermediate/high (p899).":["u18"],"copy|10–30% of clinically diagnosed AD-dementia patients lack amyloid/tau pathology at autopsy — clinical diagnosis and biological (autopsy) diagnosis are not the same thing (p908). 2018 AT(N) framework reframes AD as a biological (amyloid+tau), not purely clinical, diagnosis.":["u19"],"copy|Most common: amnestic presentation — progressive recent memory loss, often unrecognized by the patient (p900).":["u20"],"copy|Nonamnestic presentations (language, visuospatial, executive dysfunction) are more often missed/delayed in diagnosis.":["u21"],"copy|Late stages: falls, dysphagia, incontinence, immobility; death commonly from pneumonia, urosepsis, dehydration, pressure sores.":["u22"],"copy|NIA-AA dementia criteria (Table 59-1): decline from previous level that interferes with function, not explained by delirium or major psychiatric disorder; history from patient and a knowledgeable informant + objective cognitive assessment; impairment involves at least two cognitive or behavioral domains (p895).":["u23"],"copy|NIA-AA MCI criteria (Table 59-2): concern about change noted by the patient, an informant or a clinician; lower performance in ≥1 domain; preserved independence in functional abilities; not dementia (p896).":["u24"],"copy|Reversible-cause workup: meds (anticholinergic/sedating), depression, hearing loss, OSA, alcohol, vascular disease, delirium.":["u25"],"copy|Labs: B12, folate, TSH, CBC, electrolytes, LFTs, 25-OH vitamin D; HIV/syphilis if atypical; thiamine if heavy alcohol use (p906).":["u26"],"copy|Brain CT or MRI recommended in patients with documented cognitive impairment (p906).":["u27"],"copy|MCI noted in up to 15–20% of older adults; progresses to dementia at ~12–15%/year (p907).":["u28"],"copy|AChEIs (mainstay): donepezil (Aricept), galantamine (Razadyne), rivastigmine (Exelon) — the text says start low with dose increases approximately every 2 months (p911), while Table 59-7 lists drug-specific steps: donepezil 4–6 wk before 5→10 mg (the 10→23 mg step after 3 months on 10 mg) (p912), galantamine ≥4 wk per step (p912), rivastigmine ≥2 wk per oral step or 4–6 wk for the patch (p912); 18–48% show cognitive improvement, most others just have slowed decline (p911). Cautions: GI upset (take with food); bradycardia/AV block/syncope/weight loss are triggers to deprescribe.":["u29"],"copy|Memantine (Namenda) — NMDA-receptor antagonist, approved for moderate-to-severe AD; combo with an AChEI has not been shown to be superior to either drug alone (p911).":["u30"],"copy|Aducanumab — FDA accelerated approval June 2021 (p911); anti-amyloid monoclonal antibody, FDA-labeled only for MCI due to AD and mild AD (not moderate-to-severe AD) (Table 59-7, p914). One trial: 22% reduced cognitive decline (CDR-SB) over 18 months; no statistically significant benefit in the other trial. ARIA-E/ARIA-H in 34–36% of high-dose patients, ~80% of those asymptomatic (p911).":["u31"],"copy|SPRINT-MIND: SBP goal 120 mmHg (vs 140) → 19% reduction in incident MCI (p915).":["u32"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic care: exercise, cognitive/social stimulation, safety review (driving, firearms, stove), caregiver support/respite.":["u33"],"copy|Behavioral symptoms: nonpharmacologic management first-line (see Ch. 60 for detail).":["u34"],"copy|Feeding tubes are not recommended in advanced AD — they do not prolong survival or improve comfort, and have not been shown to reduce pressure sores, infection, or aspiration risk (p916).":["u35"],"recall|Recall":["u36"],"copy|row:TYPE OF DEMENTIA|TH|TYPE OF DEMENTIA":["u38"],"copy|row:TYPE OF DEMENTIA|TH|ALZHEIMER DISEASE":["u39"],"copy|row:TYPE OF DEMENTIA|TH|VASCULAR DEMENTIA":["u40"],"copy|row:TYPE OF DEMENTIA|TH|DEMENTIA WITH LEWY BODIES":["u41"],"copy|row:TYPE OF DEMENTIA|TH|FRONTOTEMPORAL DEMENTIA":["u42"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Typical Course":["u43"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Insidious onset and gradually progressive":["u44","u47"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Acute onset of cognitive impairment with some stabilization (if only one vascular event) and/or stepwise deterioration (if multiple infarcts)":["u45"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Progressive cognitive decline with fluctuating cognition, attention, and alertness":["u46"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Cognitive Symptoms":["u48"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Memory is the most commonly affected cognitive domainMay also have impairments in executive function, language, and/or visuospatial skills":["u49"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Various cognitive domains may be affected depending on the location of the clinical stroke(s) and/or severe subcortical cerebrovascular disease":["u50"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Cognitive symptoms may fluctuateMay have prominent impairment in visuospatial ability, attention, and/or executive function":["u51"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Will have early behavioral disinhibition and apathy (frontal lobe predominance) or early prominent language abnormalities (temporal lobe predominance)Deficits are chiefly noted in executive tasks with relative sparing of memory and visuospatial skills":["u52"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Other Associated Symptoms/Signs":["u53"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Some patients may have agitation and/or behavioral changes":["u54"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|May or may not have focal neurologic signs on examinationShould have evidence of relevant cerebrovascular disease by brain imaging":["u55"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|May have recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations (usually people or animals), parkinsonism (including tremor, rigidity, and postural instability), recurrent falls and syncope, rapid eye movement (REM) sleep behavior disorder, neuroleptic sensitivity, and/or delusions":["u56"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|In behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia, may have early behavioral disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, perseverative behaviors, and hyperorality":["u57"],"copy|CHAPTER 59s / HAZZARD":["u58"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 59 — Dementia Including Alzheimer Disease; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u59"],"copy|Definition & epidemiology":["u60"],"copy|Genetics & risk factors":["u61"],"copy|Pathophysiology (brief)":["u62"],"copy|Clinical presentation":["u63"],"copy|Evaluation":["u64"],"copy|Drugs":["u65"],"copy|Prevention, safety, and end-of-life":["u66"],"copy|2.":["u67"],"copy|How does APOE ε4 allele dose affect AD risk?":["u68"],"copy|One ε4 copy increases risk ~4-fold; two copies increase risk ~12-fold (p895).":["u69"],"copy|3.":["u70"],"copy|In the Framingham study, what percentage of APOE ε4 homozygotes developed AD by age 85, versus noncarriers?":["u71"],"copy|55% of ε4 homozygotes vs. 9% of noncarriers (27% for heterozygotes) (p895).":["u72"],"copy|8.":["u73"],"copy|Name the three FDA-approved acetylcholinesterase inhibitors used for AD.":["u74"],"copy|Donepezil (Aricept), galantamine (Razadyne), rivastigmine (Exelon) (p911).":["u75"],"copy|11.":["u76"],"copy|What ARIA (amyloid-related imaging abnormality) rate was seen with high-dose aducanumab, and what fraction of those cases were symptomatic?":["u77"],"copy|ARIA-E/ARIA-H occurred in 34–36% of high-dose patients; about 80% of those were asymptomatic (p911).":["u78"],"copy|12.":["u79"],"copy|In the SPRINT-MIND trial, what systolic BP target reduced incident MCI, and by how much?":["u80"],"copy|A systolic BP goal of 120 mmHg (vs. 140 mmHg) produced a 19% reduction in incident MCI (p915).":["u81"],"copy|13.":["u82"],"copy|Why are feeding tubes not recommended for patients with advanced AD at end of life?":["u83"],"copy|They do not prolong survival or increase comfort, and have not been shown to reduce risk of pressure sores, infection, or aspiration (p916).":["u84"],"copy|14.":["u85"],"copy|What is the prevalence of MCI among older adults, and what fraction progress to dementia each year?":["u86"],"copy|MCI is noted in up to 15–20% of older adults; roughly 12–15% progress to dementia each year (p907).":["u87"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, p.908 · Read source":["u88"],"copy|Must first meet diagnostic criteria for dementia (see Table 59-1).":["u89"],"copy|Answer":["u91","u92","u93","u94","u95","u96","u97"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u98"],"copy|Preserved independence, objective testing and the transition from MCI to dementia — December 2021 Q12, December 2021 Q19, December 2021 Q74, December 2021 Q75, June 2026 Q36, June 2026 Q73.":["u99"],"copy|Gradual decline, collateral history and early depressive symptoms — 2020 Q34, December 2021 Q28.":["u100"],"copy|Which test of abstract thinking is used in the MoCA — June 2026 Q82.":["u101"],"copy|Familial mutations and susceptibility to sporadic Alzheimer disease — June 2022 Q18, June 2023 Q4, May 2024 Q71, September 2024 Q10.":["u102"],"copy|Frontotemporal, Lewy body, vascular and Alzheimer presentations — June 2023 Q33, September 2024 Q15, June 2026 Q56, June 2026 Q65.":["u103"],"copy|Biomarker classification, presymptomatic disease and mixed pathology — June 2022 Q36, June 2026 Q23, June 2026 Q43.":["u104"],"copy|Which patients benefit from cholinesterase inhibitors and what treatment changes — 2020 Q5, December 2021 Q72, December 2021 Q78, June 2023 Q59, June 2026 Q5.":["u105"],"copy|When memantine is an option and how its adverse effects differ — December 2021 Q14, June 2023 Q35, September 2024 Q61.":["u106"],"copy|Donepezil, gastrointestinal tolerance, sleep disturbance and syncope — 2020 Q87, June 2022 Q39, June 2023 Q99, May 2024 Q87, June 2026 Basic Q102.":["u107"],"copy|Vascular exposure, hearing, smoking and what prevention trials actually measured — 2020 Q83, December 2021 Q24, June 2022 Q49, June 2023 Q54, June 2023 Q56, June 2025 Q61, June 2026 Q16.":["u108"],"copy|Preserved abilities as a starting point for cognitive and social activity — 2020 Q40.":["u109"],"copy|What slower decline in an anti-amyloid trial means for a patient — June 2025 Q15.":["u110"],"copy|Advanced dementia, hospice criteria, infection and the goals of treatment — 2020 Q16, December 2021 Q68, June 2022 Q46, May 2024 Q33, September 2024 Q57.":["u111"],"recognizing-cognitive-decline-in-daily-life|Recognizing cognitive decline in daily life":["u112"],"reading-the-pattern-and-the-biomarkers-together|Reading the pattern and the biomarkers together":["u113"],"interpreting-family-history-age-and-risk|Interpreting family history, age and risk":["u114"],"choosing-symptomatic-therapy-and-recognizing-toxicity|Choosing symptomatic therapy and recognizing toxicity":["u115"],"reducing-risk-supporting-function-and-interpreting-treatment-effects|Reducing risk, supporting function and interpreting treatment effects":["u116"],"care-in-advanced-dementia-and-hospice-assessment|Care in advanced dementia and hospice assessment":["u117"],"israeli-context|Israeli context — דמנציה בישראל":["u118"],"copy|Late-onset AD accounts for more than 95% of cases in the chapter's estimate (p894). Familial AD accounts for 1–5% and is one cause of younger-onset AD. Younger-onset disease can also occur without a causative familial mutation (p897).":["u6"],"copy|Aducanumab received FDA accelerated approval in June 2021 after trials in MCI due to AD or mild AD dementia (p911, Table 59-7, p914). EMERGE found 22% less worsening on CDR-SB over 18 months. ENGAGE did not show significant benefit. In the pooled 10 mg/kg groups, 35.2% had ARIA-E, 41.3% had any ARIA-E or ARIA-H, and 75.8% of participants with any ARIA were asymptomatic (EMERGE/ENGAGE safety analysis, Tables 1 and 3).":["u31"],"copy|Start with what the patient used to do, what now takes longer and who has had to take over.":["u119"],"copy|In mild cognitive impairment (MCI) there is a reported change and objective impairment in at least one domain, judged against age and education.":["u120"],"copy|Complex tasks may take longer or require a list, but the patient remains independent.":["u121"],"copy|Repeated medication or financial errors that require another person to take responsibility change the functional diagnosis.":["u122"],"copy|June 2026 Q36 tested this distinction (p895–896, Tables 59-1 and 59-2).":["u123"],"copy|A single screening score gives a level of performance.":["u124"],"copy|Interviewing the patient and someone who knows them establishes whether that level represents decline.":["u125"],"copy|An academic may start from a high baseline, and sensory loss or limited schooling may affect performance.":["u126"],"copy|June 2026 Q82 uses Harrison's example of abstract thinking: identify the relationship between two objects.":["u127"],"copy|The chapter describes the role of a MoCA in a wider assessment (p901–906).":["u128"],"copy|A gradual decline over months or years supports probable Alzheimer dementia.":["u129"],"copy|Acute fluctuation calls for evaluation of delirium and its cause, followed by reassessment of cognition after recovery.":["u130"],"copy|Depression can coexist with AD and may present with irritability, altered appetite or sleep.":["u131"],"copy|Ask about previous function and the time course before attributing a change to dementia (p895, 900–905).":["u132"],"copy|Approximately 12% to 15% of people with MCI progress to dementia each year in the studies cited.":["u133"],"copy|This is a group rate that warrants follow-up.":["u134"],"copy|June 2026 Q73 instead uses Jack et al., Table 6.":["u135"],"copy|Stage 2 allows symptoms with objective performance in the expected range.":["u136"],"copy|Stage 3 requires objectively impaired performance with independence preserved.":["u137"],"copy|The final key accepts both: the vignette supplies no normative context for its MoCA score (p907 and the exam's supplied article 4).":["u138"],"copy|Alzheimer disease usually begins with progressive loss of memory for recent events.":["u139"],"copy|Frontotemporal dementia can instead cause early language dysfunction with relative sparing of memory, or early disinhibition, loss of empathy and repetitive behavior.":["u140"],"copy|These two presentations explain the different FTD answers in June 2023 Q33 and September 2024 Q15 (p908, Table 59-5).":["u141"],"copy|Fluctuating attention, recurrent formed visual hallucinations and spontaneous parkinsonism favor Lewy body disease.":["u142"],"copy|A vascular lesion can instead produce an abrupt or stepwise change or a subcortical profile with slowed processing and poor attention.":["u143"],"copy|Relate the timing, deficits and imaging to each other.":["u144"],"copy|A lacune on a scan alone does not establish vascular dementia or lost independence.":["u145"],"copy|Under the VasCog criteria in June 2026 Q56 and Q65, vascular and Alzheimer contributions may both be present.":["u146"],"copy|The infarcts support the vascular component, while the type of tau assay and the APOE genotype matter to a biological attribution (p895, 908; supplied article 2, Boxes 2–3).":["u147"],"copy|Clinical diagnosis describes the syndrome, while biomarkers can identify pathology.":["u148"],"copy|Hazzard's 2018 research framework groups amyloid, pathological tau and evidence of neuronal injury as AT(N).":["u149"],"copy|Injury may have causes other than AD.":["u150"],"copy|June 2026 Q23 instead uses the later Jack criteria: an accurate plasma p-tau217 test is a Core 1 marker, tau PET is Core 2, and structural atrophy or neurofilament light is less specific.":["u151"],"copy|The IWG article used in Q43 reserves presymptomatic AD for a cognitively normal person with a very high lifetime risk, such as the person with Down syndrome in the vignette (p908–910; supplied articles 3 and 4).":["u152"],"copy|June 2022 Q36 accepts both the specified CSF finding and chronic ischemic MRI changes.":["u153"],"copy|The cognitive history does not establish either result by itself.":["u154"],"copy|CSF amyloid deposition is generally associated with lower Aβ42 or a lower Aβ42 to Aβ40 ratio, while AD-related phosphorylated tau rises.":["u155"],"copy|MRI can identify a coexisting vascular contribution (p906, 908–910).":["u156"],"copy|Age is the strongest established risk factor discussed in Hazzard.":["u157"],"copy|A family with younger-onset disease in successive generations raises a different question: a pathogenic mutation in APP, PSEN1 or PSEN2 can cause autosomal dominant AD.":["u158"],"copy|Among the familial mutations grouped in the chapter, PSEN1 accounts for about 78%.":["u159"],"copy|APP maps to chromosome 21, which also explains the relevance of APP dosage in Down syndrome.":["u160"],"copy|September 2024 Q10 officially cites p896.":["u161"],"copy|The mutation distribution that answers it is on p897–898 (p894–895, 897–898).":["u162"],"copy|APOE on chromosome 19 modifies susceptibility to late-onset AD.":["u163"],"copy|The ε4 allele increases risk in a dose-dependent fashion, while ε2 is relatively protective.":["u164"],"copy|An ε4 allele does not establish a diagnosis or make onset inevitable.":["u165"],"copy|May 2024 Q71 contrasts a susceptibility gene with the disease-causing mutations seen in some younger-onset families (p895, 897–898).":["u166"],"copy|Cholinesterase inhibitors raise acetylcholine at synapses.":["u167"],"copy|A patient with established Alzheimer dementia may stabilize or decline more slowly.":["u168"],"copy|Hazzard reports measurable cognitive improvement in 18% to 48% of treated patients, while most have no readily noticeable improvement.":["u169"],"copy|These agents have not been shown to delay progression from MCI to dementia.":["u170"],"copy|June 2026 Q5 asked what to tell the family, and June 2023 Q59 distinguished treatment of AD or Lewy body dementia from treatment of memory complaints alone (p910–912).":["u171"],"copy|Ask about pauses, fainting, conduction disease and weight loss before titrating.":["u172"],"copy|The medication table associates donepezil with insomnia as well as bradycardia, heart block and syncope.":["u173"],"copy|A dose taken with food may reduce gastrointestinal effects.":["u174"],"copy|Taking it fasting is the false claim in June 2023 Q99.":["u175"],"copy|June 2022 Q39 accepts the three cholinesterase inhibitors after appeal, consistent with a class concern about bradycardia.":["u176"],"copy|June 2026 Basic Q102 names Chapter 59 in the official list but gives printed p945–946 in Chapter 61.":["u177"],"copy|The donepezil adverse-effect row relevant to the tracing is on p912 (p911–913, Table 59-7).":["u178"],"copy|Memantine blocks NMDA receptors and is described for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer disease.":["u179"],"copy|In a patient unable to tolerate a cholinesterase inhibitor because of gastrointestinal effects or bradycardia, the chapter allows memantine as the initial agent.":["u180"],"copy|Headache is listed for memantine.":["u181"],"copy|Drug stage, tolerance and symptoms explain the June 2023 and September 2024 answers.":["u182"],"copy|Combining both classes has not demonstrated superiority to either alone, and additional evidence is needed for earlier AD.":["u183"],"copy|December 2021 Q14 accepted all four options after appeal, including claims that extend beyond these treatment data (p911–914).":["u184"],"copy|Midlife hypertension, obesity and physical inactivity are associated with later AD.":["u185"],"copy|Shared risk factors can accumulate.":["u186"],"copy|In the vascular-risk cohort cited in 2020 Q83, decline was faster particularly among APOE ε4 carriers.":["u187"],"copy|A genetic association does not remove opportunities to address smoking, hearing impairment or vascular risk.":["u188"],"copy|June 2023 Q56 asks about the largest weighted contribution among the midlife factors offered in the 2020 Lancet Commission: hearing loss.":["u189"],"copy|Population-attributable estimates describe a population under assumptions, not an individual response to treating hearing loss (p895–897, 915; Livingston et al., 2020, Table 2).":["u190"],"copy|Intensive blood-pressure treatment in SPRINT-MIND reduced incident MCI, a different endpoint from a conclusive reduction in dementia.":["u191"],"copy|The 2020 Commission emphasizes treating hypertension, while the review cited in June 2026 Q16 distinguishes observational associations from randomized proof that modifying a single exposure prevents dementia.":["u192"],"copy|June 2025 Q61's accepted option emphasizes inherited risk.":["u193"],"copy|Select activities the person can still perform and enjoys, adapting the physical and cognitive demands to their ability.":["u195"],"copy|Frustration with a task that depends on a lost skill is a reason to change the task.":["u196"],"copy|Exercise, social activity and cognitive engagement may offer benefit, while respite and support address caregiver strain (p915).":["u197"],"copy|Amyloid removal and a clinically important change in function are distinct endpoints.":["u198"],"copy|Hazzard describes variable clinical findings and imaging abnormalities with aducanumab.":["u199"],"copy|June 2025 Q15 asks about a later lecanemab trial.":["u200"],"copy|At 18 months both group means worsened on CDR-SB, by 1.21 points with lecanemab and 1.66 with placebo.":["u201"],"copy|The treatment difference represents slower decline.":["u202"],"copy|It does not establish that an individual will improve from baseline (p911, 914; van Dyck et al., Table 2).":["u203"],"copy|Progressive dysphagia, poor intake and infection are common in advanced disease.":["u204"],"copy|Discuss prognosis and the patient's goals with the caregiver.":["u205"],"copy|In Mitchell's advanced dementia article, FAST 7c entails loss of independent ambulation.":["u206"],"copy|Hospice guidelines combine advanced functional decline with at least one qualifying complication.":["u207"],"copy|In the circumstances specified in its table, substantial weight loss or serum albumin below 2.5 g/dL can document impaired nutrition.":["u208"],"copy|These criteria estimate eligibility and cannot predict precisely whether an individual will die within six months.":["u209"],"copy|The September 2024 question supplies the advanced stage and asks which complication qualifies.":["u210"],"copy|May 2024 Q33 names Mitchell in its stem but the sheet gives X for chapter and page (p916; Mitchell, Table 1).":["u211"],"copy|When pneumonia occurs, consider relief of suffering and possible prolongation of life separately.":["u212"],"copy|Antibiotic treatment was associated with longer survival in an observational study, while greater comfort did not follow.":["u213"],"copy|Eating problems and infections are the common complications in June 2022 Q46.":["u214"],"copy|Tube feeding has not been shown to extend survival, improve comfort or reduce aspiration in advanced AD.":["u215"],"copy|Hand feeding as tolerated and a simplified regimen can align care with comfort (p916; Mitchell, clinical complications).":["u216"],"copy|מי מקבל את ההחלטה הרפואית?":["u217"],"copy|בודקים אם האדם מבין את ההחלטה המסוימת ויכול להביע את רצונו לאחר שהמידע הונגש לו.":["u218"],"copy|אם תמיכה מאפשרת לו להחליט, ההחלטה נשארת בידיו.":["u219"],"copy|בעודו מסוגל להבין, אדם בגיר יכול לקבוע בייפוי כוח מתמשך מי יטפל בעתיד בענייניו הרפואיים ובאילו עניינים.":["u220"],"copy|אפוטרופוס מתמנה בצו של בית המשפט, שיכול להגביל את המינוי לעניין מסוים.":["u221"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ״אפוטרופסות״, 30.11.2023, טבלת אפשרויות המינוי והסעיפים על ייפוי כוח מתמשך וקבלת החלטות נתמכת, בעקבות תיקון 18 לחוק הכשרות המשפטית והאפוטרופסות.":["u222"],"copy|ייפוי כוח שנערך מראש.":["u223"],"copy|בדרך הרגילה, כשהממנה חדל להבין בעניין שלגביו ניתן ייפוי הכוח, מיופה הכוח מודיע לאפוטרופוס הכללי ומצרף את המסמכים הנדרשים.":["u224"],"copy|במקרה רפואי דחוף וחריג שבו ההמתנה לאישור תעכב את הטיפול, ההנחיה מאפשרת לקבל הסכמה ממיופה הכוח לאחר שהצוות התרשם שהמטופל אינו מסוגל לתת הסכמה מדעת ושייפוי הכוח חל על הטיפול.":["u225"],"copy|מציגים את המסמך, מתחייבים לפעול בהקדם לקבלת האישור ומתעדים ברשומה.":["u226"],"copy|משרד הבריאות והאפוטרופוס הכללי, ״הפעלת ייפוי כוח מתמשך בבי״ח במקרים דחופים״, 07.08.2023, עמ׳ 1–2.":["u227"],"copy|מקבל החלטות זמני.":["u228"],"copy|תיקון 16 לחוק זכויות החולה מסדיר מסלול לטיפול רפואי דחוף בבגיר שאינו יכול לתת הסכמה מדעת גם לאחר התאמות רלוונטיות.":["u229"],"copy|רופא מומחה קובע את הדחיפות ואת יכולת המטופל להסכים.":["u230"],"copy|העובד הסוציאלי בודק מינויים, ייפוי כוח והנחיות קודמות, ומברר את הסכמת המשפחה.":["u231"],"copy|אם אין הנחיה קודמת מתאימה, סדר הקדימויות הוא בן או בת זוג, ילד, הורה ואח, ובני המשפחה יכולים להסכים על בחירת בן משפחה אחר.":["u232"],"copy|המינוי מתועד ומאושר במוסד הרפואי לתקופה של עד שישה חודשים.":["u233"],"copy|לפני כל טיפול בודקים מחדש אם המטופל מסוגל להסכים בעצמו.":["u234"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, חטיבת הרפואה, ״מקבל החלטות זמני לעניינים רפואיים״, 15.02.2024, סעיפים 1–4, 7 ונספחים א׳–ב׳, לפי סעיפים 14א–14ג לחוק זכויות החולה.":["u235"],"copy|נהיגה.":["u236"],"copy|הרופא מעריך אם הפגיעה הקוגניטיבית או התפקודית יוצרת בפועל סיכון בנהיגה, בהתחשב בחומרה, בתפקוד ובסוג הרישיון.":["u237"],"copy|כשמחליטים לדווח למרב״ד, מזהירים את הנהג, ממליצים להפסיק לנהוג עד הבדיקה ומתעדים את ההסבר.":["u238"],"copy|הדיווח עצמו אינו פוסל מיד את הרישיון.":["u239"],"copy|לאחר הבדיקה המרב״ד ממליץ לרשות הרישוי.":["u240"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, חוזר מנכ״ל 6/2023, סעיפים 4–5, 7, 10–11.":["u241"],"copy|העדכון מ־24.08.2023 ביטל את הטופס שצורף לחוזר והפנה לטופס המעודכן בפורטל המרב״ד.":["u242"],"copy|רצף הטיפול והמשפחה.":["u243"],"copy|התוכנית הלאומית משנת 2013 המליצה להרחיב גישה לאבחון בקהילה ובמרפאות זיכרון, להכין לאחר האבחון תוכנית טיפול עם האדם ועם משפחתו, ולתאם את שירותי הבריאות והרווחה.":["u244"],"copy|בשלבים מתקדמים היא הציעה לשלב טיפול פליאטיבי בבית ולהכין את המשפחה לסיבוכים ולהחלטות הצפויות.":["u245"],"copy|מרכזי יום, נופשונים וייעוץ לבני המשפחה נועדו לתת מענה גם לצורכי המטפלים.":["u246"],"copy|התוכנית הלאומית להתמודדות עם מחלת האלצהיימר ודמנציות אחרות, תקציר מנהלים, 2013, עמ׳ ii–iv, סעיפים 2–4.":["u247"],"copy|שני נתונים מישראל והאוכלוסייה שאליה הם מתייחסים.":["u248"],"copy|ברוקדייל דיווח ב־2024 שחולים מתחת לגיל 65 היו פחות מ־2% מכלל החולים בדמנציה בישראל.":["u249"],"copy|במחקר נפרד שפורסם ב־2015 רואיינו 117 בני משפחה שטיפלו בזקנים עם דמנציה מתקדמת שחיו בבית.":["u250"],"copy|74% מהמרואיינים תיארו את עומס הטיפול ככבד או כבד מאוד.":["u251"],"copy|הנתון השני מתאר את המשפחות שרואיינו במחקר הקהילתי.":["u252"],"copy|ברוקדייל, דמ־24־027, תקציר הפרסום, ״רקע״.":["u253"],"copy|ברוקדייל, דמ־15־652, תקציר הפרסום, פסקת השיטה והממצא על עומס המשפחה.":["u254"],"copy|Of 1,029 high-dose participants, 425 (41.3%) had ARIA-E or ARIA-H, including 362 (35.2%) with ARIA-E.":["u78"],"copy|Among the 425 with any ARIA, 103 (24.2%) had symptoms and 322 (75.8%) did not (p911, EMERGE/ENGAGE integrated safety analysis, Tables 1 and 3).":["u78"],"copy|AChEIs (mainstay): donepezil (Aricept), galantamine (Razadyne), rivastigmine (Exelon) — the text says start low with dose increases approximately every 2 months (p911), while Table 59-7 lists drug-specific steps: donepezil 4–6 wk before 5→10 mg (the 10→23 mg step after 3 months on 10 mg) (p912), galantamine ≥4 wk per step (p912), rivastigmine ≥2 wk per oral step or 4–6 wk for the initial patch step, with a maximum of 13.3 mg/24 h (Table 59-7, p913); 18–48% show cognitive improvement, most others have a plateau or slower cognitive decline (p911). Cautions: GI upset (take with food); bradycardia/AV block/syncope/weight loss are triggers to deprescribe.":["u29"],"copy|Aducanumab received FDA accelerated approval in June 2021 after trials in MCI due to AD or mild AD dementia (p911, Table 59-7, p914). EMERGE found 22% less worsening on CDR-SB over 18 months. ENGAGE did not show significant benefit. Hazzard reports MRI abnormalities (ARIA-E or ARIA-H) in 34–36% of high-dose participants, with about 80% of affected participants asymptomatic (p911).Current practice (not the book): the pooled trial safety analysis found ARIA-E in 35.2% and any ARIA in 41.3% of the 10 mg/kg group. Of those with any ARIA, 75.8% had no reported symptoms — Salloway et al., EMERGE/ENGAGE safety analysis, Tables 1 and 3.":["u31"],"copy|Hazzard reports ARIA in 34–36% of high-dose participants.":["u255"],"copy|Of those with ARIA, approximately 80% were asymptomatic, so about 20% were symptomatic (p911).":["u256"],"copy|CHAPTER 59 / HAZZARD":["u58"],"table-59-5|Table 59-5":["u37"],"copy|The 2024 Commission states that overall risk remains modifiable irrespective of APOE status (p915; Livingston et al., 2024, p573; supplied article 5).":["u194"],"copy|June 2023 Q33 · September 2024 Q15":["u90"]}}
